{"grade":"G10","deck_count":14,"decks":[{"grade":"G10","subject":"MATH","unit_number":1,"title":"Relations and Functions","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Relation","body":"Pairs elements of A with B. Not every relation is a function.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Function rule","body":"Each input has exactly one output.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Domain and range","body":"Domain: allowed inputs. Range: actual outputs.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"f(x)=x²+1. Domain: all real x. f(2)=5.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"x ↦ ±√x is not a function (two outputs).","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"BIOLOGY","unit_number":1,"title":"Virus — Structure & Replication","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Structure","body":"Capsid (protein coat) encloses DNA or RNA; some have envelope and spikes.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Shapes","body":"Spherical (e.g. coronavirus), helical (e.g. influenza), complex (bacteriophage).","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Replication steps","body":"Attachment → entry → replication → assembly → release.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Why a host?","body":"Viruses lack machinery to grow alone; they hijack host cells.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Key facts","body":"Acellular, microscopic, infect living organisms, genetic material DNA or RNA.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"BIOLOGY","unit_number":2,"title":"Genetics — Heredity & Variation","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"What is genetics?","body":"Genetics is the study of genes, heredity and variation in living organisms.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"DNA structure","body":"A pairs with T; G pairs with C. DNA is a double helix.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Gene & chromosome","body":"Gene: DNA segment that controls a trait. Humans: 23 pairs of chromosomes.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Genotype vs phenotype","body":"Genotype: genetic makeup (AA, Aa, aa). Phenotype: observable trait.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Dominant vs recessive","body":"Dominant (A) shows with one copy; recessive (a) needs two.","accent":"#FBBF24"},{"n":6,"heading":"Punnett example","body":"Aa × Aa → AA, Aa, Aa, aa → phenotypic ratio 3:1.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":7,"heading":"Mendel’s laws","body":"Dominance, segregation, independent assortment.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":8,"heading":"Key point","body":"Genes are DNA segments; traits are inherited from parents.","accent":"#F472B6"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"CHEMISTRY","unit_number":1,"title":"Atomic Structure and Periodicity","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Configuration","body":"Electrons fill 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s…","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Trends","body":"Radius decreases across period; IE generally increases.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Valence","body":"Outer electrons determine bonding.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"Na: 1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹ → one valence electron.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Noble gases have full outer shells.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"CHEMISTRY","unit_number":2,"title":"Chemical Bonding","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Ionic vs covalent","body":"Transfer vs share.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Lewis","body":"Dots show valence electrons.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"VSEPR","body":"Shapes: linear, bent, tetrahedral…","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"H₂O bent; CO₂ linear.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"NH₃ trigonal pyramidal.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"CHEMISTRY","unit_number":3,"title":"Stoichiometry","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Mole","body":"1 mol = 6.02×10²³ particles.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Molar mass","body":"g/mol from periodic table.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Limiting reactant","body":"Runs out first → limits product.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"2H₂+O₂→2H₂O; 4 mol H₂ need 2 mol O₂.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Moles = mass / molar mass.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"CHEMISTRY","unit_number":4,"title":"Acids, Bases and pH","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Arrhenius","body":"Acid→H⁺; base→OH⁻ in water.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"pH","body":"pH=−log[H⁺]. Acidic <7; basic >7.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Neutralization","body":"Acid+base→salt+water.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"0.01 M HCl → pH=2.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Strong acids fully ionize.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"PHYSICS","unit_number":1,"title":"Mechanics — Forces","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Newton 1","body":"Net force zero → constant velocity.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Newton 2","body":"F=ma.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Newton 3","body":"Equal and opposite forces.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"m=2 kg, a=3 → F=6 N.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Net force zero ⇒ a=0.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"PHYSICS","unit_number":2,"title":"Energy and Work","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Work","body":"W=F·d (along displacement).","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"KE","body":"½mv².","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"GPE","body":"mgh near Earth.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"m=2, v=3 → KE=9 J.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Raise 1 kg by 2 m (g≈10): GPE≈20 J.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"PHYSICS","unit_number":3,"title":"Waves and Sound","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"v=fλ","body":"Speed = frequency × wavelength.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Types","body":"Transverse vs longitudinal.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Sound","body":"Needs a medium.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"f=50, λ=2 → v=100 m/s.","accent":"#34D399"},{"n":5,"heading":"Try this","body":"Higher pitch → higher frequency.","accent":"#FBBF24"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"MATH","unit_number":2,"title":"Polynomials","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Degree","body":"Highest power with nonzero coefficient.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Factor theorem","body":"If f(a)=0 then (x−a) is a factor.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"x²−5x+6=(x−2)(x−3).","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Try this","body":"Degree of 4x³−x+7 is 3.","accent":"#34D399"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"MATH","unit_number":3,"title":"Exponential and Log","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Log definition","body":"log_b a = c ⇔ b^c = a.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Log laws","body":"log(xy)=log x+log y.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"log₂ 32 = 5.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Try this","body":"log 100 = 2.","accent":"#34D399"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"MATH","unit_number":4,"title":"Trigonometry","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"SOH-CAH-TOA","body":"sin=opp/hyp; cos=adj/hyp; tan=opp/adj.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Identity","body":"sin²θ+cos²θ=1.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"3-4-5: sin=3/5.","accent":"#F472B6"},{"n":4,"heading":"Try this","body":"tanθ=sinθ/cosθ.","accent":"#34D399"}]},{"grade":"G10","subject":"MATH","unit_number":5,"title":"Statistics","type":"illustrated_cards","slides":[{"n":1,"heading":"Mean / median / mode","body":"Average; middle; most frequent.","accent":"#22D3EE"},{"n":2,"heading":"Worked idea","body":"2,3,3,7: mean=3.75, median=3, mode=3.","accent":"#A78BFA"},{"n":3,"heading":"Try this","body":"Median resists outliers better than mean.","accent":"#F472B6"}]}]}
